  <!-- ═══ INSURANCE PROTECTION GAP ═══ -->
  <section id="insurance-gap" class="py-16 border-b border-ink/8">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6">
      <h2 class="font-serif font-semibold text-xl md:text-[1.5rem] tracking-tight text-ink leading-tight mb-6">A record burn the insurance market barely registers</h2>

      <div class="space-y-6 text-body text-[15px] leading-[1.7]">
        <p>
          EFFIS mapping records 11.05 million hectares burned across Europe between 2006 and 2025, yet no European wildfire has produced an insured loss exceeding $1 billion. Portugal's 2017 season, the continent's most destructive on record at the time, caused approximately €1 billion in total economic damage, of which €244 million was covered by insurance (Aon). Spain's 2025 season, the worst in three decades, produced close to €5 billion in economic losses but well under €1 billion in insured payments (Morningstar DBRS). By comparison, the January 2025 Los Angeles fires alone generated insured losses estimated at $40 billion, per Swiss Re Institute sigma records, the costliest wildfire event ever recorded.
        </p>
        <p>
          The gap is partly structural. European wildfire burns primarily through forest, scrubland and agricultural land rather than the dense residential concentrations of southern California. Four of the five most fire-burdened countries in the burn-intensity index are in the Western Balkans, where non-life insurance penetration runs at roughly 1.5–2% of GDP (World Bank / Swiss Re sigma data), a fraction of Western European levels. Even in Spain and Portugal, where household property insurance take-up is higher, the most fire-exposed land is rural and frequently uninsured or excluded from standard property policies.
        </p>
        <p>
          The result is a protection gap that works in both directions: the countries burning the most carry the least insurance, and the insurance market has little historical loss data on which to price European wildfire exposure.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

