  <!-- ═══ METHODOLOGY ═══ -->
  <section id="methodology" class="py-16 bg-bg-paper border-b border-ink/8">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6">
      <h2 class="font-serif font-semibold text-xl md:text-[1.5rem] tracking-tight text-ink leading-tight mb-8">Methodology</h2>
      <div class="space-y-6 text-body text-[15px] leading-[1.7] italic">
        <p>
          All burned-area figures are satellite-mapped totals from the European Forest Fire Information System (EFFIS), part of the EU's Copernicus Emergency Management Service, which maps fires of approximately 30 hectares and larger using MODIS and VIIRS imagery. Annual country totals for 2006–2026 were retrieved programmatically from EFFIS's seasonal trend reporting service on 30 July 2026; each figure in the dataset carries its retrieval timestamp. EFFIS-mapped totals differ from national agency statistics, which use different methods and thresholds; EFFIS is used exclusively throughout for cross-country comparability. Rankings cover 2006–2025; 2026 figures are season-to-date and reported separately. Burn intensity divides cumulative burned area by national land area (World Bank), counting land that burned more than once each time it burned; it does not indicate that a fixed quarter of any country's territory has burned exactly once. Decade-over-decade change compares 2016–2025 with 2006–2015 and is reported only where the first-decade total reached at least 1,000 hectares. Ukraine is excluded from twenty-year rankings because EFFIS coverage begins in 2020 and its recent totals are substantially war-affected. Small monitored countries with no mapped fires of 30 hectares or larger in a given year record zero for that year.
        </p>
        <p>
          Fatality figures are compiled per event from official tolls and contemporaneous primary reporting; the full itemised event list, with sources, is included in the downloadable dataset. Where reported tolls vary, the most widely cited figure is used and the range noted (Greece 2007: reported range 78–85; Portugal 2017: government total of 117 across the June and October events). Where season totals could not be fully itemised, the verified floor is used, so country totals and the 400-death aggregate are minimums. Events with no verified toll are excluded rather than estimated. Deaths per million uses 2024 population estimates.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

